### How it was played (two modes)

1. **Deterministic stepping** (`?manual`: the page's own rAF loop is off, `SMB.step(n)` advances n logic frames, `SMB.Input.set()` presses buttons). All feel measurements, mechanic probes and the complete run use this mode, via `tools/smb_drive.py`. It is exact and repeatable; the game is deterministic.
2. **Real time** (`tools/realtime_check.py`): normal page, rAF loop, real keyboard events from Playwright, music element playing, video recorded (`evidence/realtime/realtime_run.webm`). This checks that the live loop runs at 60 Hz, that real key events work, that music starts and that the hurry-up sequence plays in order.

A full playthrough was produced by a seeded bot (`tools/run_bot.py`, `tools/smb_bot.js`): it plays 1-1 from the title with keyboard inputs only. Seed 2000 reaches the flag and the castle on one life. Its input log `win_1-1_seed2000.json` replays it exactly.

### What I could not do

- **I did not hear anything** and did not see the game on a real monitor. Sound is judged from (a) which `SMB.Sound` calls fired for each event, (b) decoding all 30 audio files (length, peak, silence, loop seam, pitch track). "Sounds right" is therefore **not verified**, only "the right cue is triggered and the file is non-silent".
- Input latency as a human feels it is not measured; only logic frames.
- Comparison with the original 1-1 is from my memory of the NES game (no reference was supplied). Those comparisons are marked *assumed*.

### Instrumentation (so nobody mistakes these for normal play)

- `probes_enemies.py`, `measure_feel.py`: columns 0..26 of 1-1 are emptied above the floor so enemies/blocks do not disturb the test.
- `probes_items.py`, `probes_misc.py`, `probes_extra.py`, `panorama.py`: Mario is teleported, game time is set (fireworks digit, hurry, time-up), enemies spawned by `SMB.makeEnemy`, or scripted bumps are made at fixed intervals.
- Everything in section 3 headed "complete run" and "real time" uses **no** teleport or state edit.

## 2. Experience in one paragraph

Build-1 opens straight from `index.html`, shows a convincing SMB1-style title screen (logo, TOP score, mushroom cursor), starts with Enter, and plays like the NES game: Mario's walk/run speeds equal the NES numbers, the jump and skid read right, the 1-1 layout in the stitched panorama matches the original as I remember it, the enemies, blocks, power-ups, pipe to the coin room, flagpole, castle walk, time tally and fireworks all work, and death, pit, time-up and game over all lead back to the title and a fresh game. I could play it title → flagpole → castle → clear screen on one life. I found **no blocker**. The one thing that deviates from SMB1 feel enough to matter is that a standing jump does not reach the top of a 4-tile pipe (D1). The rest is polish.

